    "locked": false,
    "maintainer_can_modify": true,
    "title": "refactor : Reorder CBlockIndex members to save 8 bytes",
    "user": {
      "login": "bartoli",
      "id": 4445979,
      "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjQ0NDU5Nzk=",
      "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4445979?v=4",
      "gravatar_id": "",
      "url": "https://api.github.com/users/bartoli",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/bartoli",
      "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bartoli/followers",
      "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bartoli/following%7B/other_user%7D",
      "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bartoli/gists%7B/gist_id%7D",
      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bartoli/starred%7B/owner%7D%7B/repo%7D",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bartoli/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bartoli/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bartoli/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bartoli/events%7B/privacy%7D",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bartoli/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "site_admin": false,
      "name": null,
      "patch_url": null
    },
    "body": "It seems we can save 8 bytes per CBlockIndex instance by reordering one class member. Moving one 8-byte field seems to avoid losing 2x 4 bytes to structure padding on a 64-bit build (on a 32-bit build, there is no gain).\n\nI could not exactly measure the runtime gain from just this (smaller than RAM usage variation between 2 launches), but I confirmed there was bout as many instances of CBlockIndex as blocks in the chain (increasing CBlockIndex size by 320 bytes increases RAM usage by ~300MB), so saving 8 bytes per instance should save about 8MB of RAM at current block height.\n\nThe gain is small, but it seems like it has no cost/risk, since that structure is not used for serialization, and just reordering here should have no side effects.\n\nI saw a few other types that could gain a few bytes in size by doing something similar (for example, CTxIn), but they have far fewer instances in memory to make the change worth it.\n\n",
